@node	5.2, 5.3, 5.1, Chapter 5
@section A Register-Machine Simulator

In order to gain a good understanding of the design of register machines, we
must test the machines we design to see if they perform as expected.  One way
to test a design is to hand-simulate the operation of the controller, as in
@ref{Exercise 5.5}.  But this is extremely tedious for all but the simplest
machines.  In this section we construct a simulator for machines described in
the register-machine language.  The simulator is an OCaml module whose
interface builds a model of the machine and runs it:

@quotation

@example ocaml
make_machine ~registers ~operations ~controller
@end example

@noindent
assembles the controller and returns a model of the machine with the given
registers and operations, checking every register, operation, and label
before the machine exists.

@example ocaml
set_register m "a" (Int 206)
@end example

@noindent
stores a value in a simulated register of the given machine.

@example ocaml
get_register m "a"
@end example

@noindent
reads the contents of a simulated register of the given machine.

@example ocaml
start m
@end example

@noindent
steps the machine from the current program counter to the end of the
controller sequence.
@end quotation

@noindent
As an example of how these operations are used, we can build a model of the
@abbr{GCD} machine of @ref{5.1.1} as follows:

@example ocaml
let gcd_machine =
  make_machine
    ~registers:[ "a"; "b"; "t" ]
    ~operations:arith_operations
    gcd_controller
;;
(* the assembler checked every register, label, and operation before
   the machine exists *)
@end example

@noindent
To compute @abbr{GCD}s with this machine, we set the input registers, start
the machine, and examine the result when the simulation terminates:

@example ocaml
let* () = set_register gcd_machine "a" (Int 206) in
let* () = set_register gcd_machine "b" (Int 40) in
let* () = start gcd_machine in
get_register gcd_machine "a"
;;
(* = Ok (Int 2): each store answers Ok (), and the stopped machine
   answers 2 *)
@end example

@noindent
This computation will run much more slowly than a @code{gcd} procedure written
in OCaml, because we will simulate low-level machine instructions, such as
@code{Assign}, by much more complex operations.

Machine fixtures run the same way through @code{read_fixture} and
@code{run_fixture}: @code{print} writes its operand and a newline, and after
the controller stops each declared register is written as @code{name: value}.
The @abbr{GCD} fixture answers

@example
a: 2
b: 0
t: 0
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.7}Exercise 5.7:} Use the simulator to test the
machines you designed in @ref{Exercise 5.4}: assemble each controller with
@code{make_machine}, load the inputs with @code{set_register}, run it with
@code{start}, and read the answer with @code{get_register}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{Exercise 5.7a is added by this edition and extends exercise 5.7;
SICP numbers stop at 5.7.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.7a}Exercise 5.7a:} Pair each simulated run
of Exercise 5.7 with a direct host computation of the same function as
an oracle: run the recursive Fibonacci machine of @ref{Figure 5.12}
and the iterative factorial machine of Exercise 5.1 on a shared set
of arguments, compute each answer directly on the host with the
