@subsection The Machine Model

The machine model built by @code{make} holds an array register file, a
program-counter ref, a test flag, a save/restore stack with its monitors, and
the assembled controller.  The machine is polymorphic in its word: the
machines of 5.1 and 5.2 hold @code{value}s, @ref{5.3} holds tagged memory
words, and the evaluator and compiler of @ref{5.4} and @ref{5.5} hold
evaluator words.  One simulator runs them all.  Each word family says how to
make a label word, read one back, render a word for traces, and what an
unassigned register holds:

@example ocaml
type 'w words =
  @{ label : string -> 'w
  ; to_label : 'w -> string option
  ; show : 'w -> string
  ; unassigned : 'w
  @}
@end example

@noindent
The words of the 5.1 and 5.2 machines are integers, floats, Booleans, strings,
labels held as data, and the unassigned word:

@example ocaml
type value =
  | Int of int
  | Float of float
  | Bool of bool
  | Str of string
  | Addr of string
  | Unassigned
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.13}Figure 5.13:} @math{\downarrow} The machine model: the
register file beside the assembled program.

@example ocaml
type 'w machine =
  @{ words : 'w words
  ; names : string array
  ; contents : 'w array
  ; source : 'w program
  ; executable : 'w executable array
  ; label_table : (string, int) Hashtbl.t
  ; pc_ref : int ref
  ; flag : bool option ref
  ; stack : 'w list ref
  ; depth : int ref
  ; pushes : int ref
  ; max_depth : int ref
  ; count : int ref
  @}
@end example

@end quotation

@noindent
The test flag is an ordinary ref cell, initially unset: a branch reads it and
fails with the typed error when no test has yet set it.  The program counter
is the machine's own index into the instruction array, which labels assemble
to; the record keeps the register file and the array side by side.

@subsubheading Registers

A register is a slot of the register file, named at declaration.  Reading or
assigning an undeclared register is the typed @code{Unknown_register} failure,
raised by @code{assemble} before execution and by @code{set_register} and
@code{get_register} at once.@footnote{The book's machine keeps its registers
in a table that grows as @code{allocate-register} runs, with @code{flag} and
@code{pc} among the entries.  This edition fixes the register file at
declaration and keeps the program counter and the flag outside it, so the
scan of @ref{Exercise 5.13} names only the controller's own registers.}

@subsubheading The stack

The stack saves words on @code{Save} and restores them on @code{Restore}, last
in first out.  Restoring an empty stack is a typed failure.  @ref{5.3} will
implement this same stack as ordinary list structure; here it is part of the
machine model, with monitors that count the pushes and the deepest depth
reached (@ref{5.2.4}).

@subsubheading The basic machine

@code{make} assembles the controller and checks it against the declared
registers and operations: every named register is declared, every operation
is installed with the kind its instruction requires (@code{Value_op} for
@code{Assign_op}, @code{Test_op} for @code{Test}, @code{Action_op} for
@code{Perform}), and every label referenced is defined.  The 5.2 machine adds
the stack monitors and @code{print} to the table; those write into the
machine's transcript, which @code{transcript} reads back in order.

@node	5.2.2, 5.2.3, 5.2.1, 5.2
@subsection The Assembler

The assembler turns the controller list into an executable program.  It
extracts the labels first, pairing each label with the index of the
instruction it names (a label at the end names the stop index, one past the
last instruction), and rejects a label defined twice, which is the resolution
of @ref{Exercise 5.8}:

@example ocaml
val assemble : 'w instruction list -> ('w program, error) result

type 'w program =
  @{ code : 'w instruction array
  ; labels : (string * int) list
  @}
@end example

@noindent
Much useful analysis of the controller can be performed without knowing the
actual contents of machine registers.  The assembler replaces references to
labels by indices into the instruction array, and @code{make} checks every
register and operation up front, so execution never meets an unknown name
from a well-formed controller.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.8}Exercise 5.8:} The following controller is
ambiguous, because the label @code{here} is defined more than once:

@example ocaml
[ Label "start"
; Goto "here"
; Label "here"
; Assign ("a", Const (Int 3))
; Goto "there"
; Label "here"
; Assign ("a", Const (Int 4))
; Goto "there"
; Label "there"
]
@end example

With the simulator as written, what will the contents of register @code{a} be
when control reaches @code{there}?  Modify the assembler's label scan
procedure so that the assembler will signal an error if the same label name
is used to indicate two different locations.  (In this edition the scan
already does: @code{assemble} reports the typed @code{Bad_instruction}
failure that the label @code{here} is defined twice, so nothing is assembled
and the machine is never started.)
@end quotation

@node	5.2.3, 5.2.4, 5.2.2, 5.2
@subsection Generating Execution Procedures for Instructions

@code{step} executes one instruction and answers whether the controller has
run off its end; @code{start} steps to the end.  Like the @code{analyze}
procedure in the evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}, execution dispatches on the type
of instruction.  The program counter, the test flag, and the stack carry the
machine state between steps.

@subsubheading @code{Assign} instructions

@code{Assign (register, source)} loads the register with a constant word, the
contents of another register, or a label word.  @code{Assign_op} computes a
@code{Value_op} on its inputs first.  An operation input is a constant or a
register's contents; a label in operand position is refused at assembly (see
@ref{Exercise 5.9}), while a plain @code{Assign} of a label is how
@code{continue} receives return addresses.

@subsubheading @code{Test}, @code{branch}, and @code{goto} instructions

@code{Test (operation, inputs)} runs a @code{Test_op} and sets the flag.
@code{Branch label} continues at the label when the flag is set and falls
through otherwise; a branch with no test before it is the typed
@code{Branch_without_test} failure.  @code{Goto label} jumps to the
assembled index; @code{Goto_reg register} reads a label word from the
register and jumps there, which is how a subroutine returns.

@subsubheading Other instructions

@code{Save register} pushes the register's word and counts one push;
@code{Restore register} pops the top word into the register.  @code{Perform
(operation, inputs)} runs an @code{Action_op} for its effect.  @code{Label}
executes nothing.  @code{pc} reads the next instruction's index, with the
length of the code array meaning the machine has stopped; @code{executed}
counts the instructions stepped since the machine was made or restarted;
@code{goto_label} enters a controller at a label, and @code{restart} resets
the program counter, the flag, and the stack.

@subsubheading Execution procedures for subexpressions

An operand @code{Const word} is the word itself; @code{Reg name} is the
register's contents; @code{Label_ref label} is the label's word.  Operands
are read when the instruction executes, so an @code{Assign_op} always sees
current register contents.  The assembler checks the shapes; execution
reports the failures: unknown registers, unknown operations, unknown labels,
empty-stack restores, and branches without tests.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.9}Exercise 5.9:} The typed @code{source} admits a
label in any operand position, but an operation input must be a register or a
constant: a label is a legal source only for a plain @code{Assign}, which is
how @code{continue} receives return addresses.  Write @code{check_operands},
which scans every @code{Assign_op}, @code{Test}, and @code{Perform} and
refuses a @code{Label_ref} input at assembly, before execution, and arrange
for @code{make_machine} to run that check before assembling.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.10}Exercise 5.10:} Design a new syntax for
writing controllers, with statements for assignment, test, branch, goto,
save, restore, and perform over register, number, label, and operation-call
terms.  Translate each statement into exactly one simulator instruction,
refusing what the machine cannot run, and show that the new syntax can be
installed without changing any part of the simulator below the translation.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.11}Exercise 5.11:} When we introduced @code{Save}
and @code{Restore} we used the simulator's untagged stack, in which
@code{Save "y"; Save "x"; Restore "y"} leaves @code{y} holding the word saved
from @code{x}.  Design two more disciplines that leave the simulator
unchanged: a tagged discipline that files the register's name with the word,
and a per-register discipline that gives each register its own stack.  What
does the same sequence leave in @code{y} under each?  Check the Fibonacci
machine under all three.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.12}Exercise 5.12:} The simulator can be used to help
analyze a controller.  Over the assembled typed instructions of the Fibonacci
machine of @ref{Figure 5.12}, list the unique instructions by kind, the
entry-point register (the goto-to-register target), the stacked registers,
and the per-register sources.  Do the same for the factorial machine of
@ref{Figure 5.11}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.13}Exercise 5.13:} Modify the simulator so that it
accepts machine descriptions in which there is no @code{registers} list:
derive the registers from the controller with the simulator's own register
scan (@code{instruction_registers}), keeping first-use order and dropping
duplicates.  Every register the controller names is then declared by
construction.  Check the GCD and Fibonacci machines with no register list at
all.
@end quotation

