@subsection Monitoring Machine Performance

Simulation is useful not only for verifying the correctness of a proposed
machine design but also for measuring the machine's performance.  Every 5.2
machine carries the stack monitors: @code{initialize_stack} empties the stack
and zeroes its monitors, and @code{print_stack_statistics} writes the monitor
line into the machine's transcript.  Saving two words and restoring one, then
printing the statistics and the register, gives the transcript

@example
total-pushes = 2 maximum-depth = 2
7
@end example

@noindent
@code{stack_statistics} reads the same counters directly as total pushes and
maximum depth, @code{stack_depth} reads the words currently saved, and
@code{executed} counts the instructions stepped.  @ref{Exercise 5.15} through
@ref{Exercise 5.19} describe other useful monitoring and debugging features
that a @code{Monitor} over the frozen simulator provides: instruction counts
(fib 3 takes 51 instructions, fib 6 takes 281), constructor-notation traces
with labels, register-write traces, and breakpoints with proceed and cancel.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.14}Exercise 5.14:} Measure the number of pushes and
the maximum stack depth required to compute @math{{n!}} for various small values of
@math{n} using the factorial machine shown in @ref{Figure 5.11}.  From your data
determine formulas in terms of @math{n} for the total number of push operations
and the maximum stack depth used in computing @math{{n!}} for any @math{{n > 1}}. Note
that each of these is a linear function of @math{n} and is thus determined by two
constants.  In order to get the statistics printed, you will have to augment
the factorial machine with @code{Perform ("initialize-stack", [])} before the
recursion and @code{Perform ("print-stack-statistics", [])} at
@code{fact-done}.  Both counters are @code{2n - 2} for @code{n > 1}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.15}Exercise 5.15:} Add @newterm{instruction counting}
to the register machine simulation.  That is, drive the simulator one
@code{step} at a time and count one per executed instruction, with a message
that prints the value of the instruction count and resets the count to zero.
Fib 3 executes 51 instructions and fib 6 executes 281, the same 281 the
hand transcription of @ref{5.1} counted step by step; after the reset the
count reads 0.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.16}Exercise 5.16:} Augment the simulator to provide
for @newterm{instruction tracing}.  That is, before each instruction is
executed, the monitor renders the assembled instruction in constructor
notation.  Tracing gcd(12, 8) prints fourteen lines, two full loop passes
plus the final test and taken branch; the same machine on (20, 14) with
tracing off prints nothing but answers 2.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.17}Exercise 5.17:} Extend the instruction tracing of
@ref{Exercise 5.16} so that before printing an instruction, the monitor prints
any labels that immediately precede that instruction in the controller
sequence, the assembled program keeping each label with its index.  Each pass
through the loop opens with @code{fib-loop:}, the returns open
@code{afterfib-n-1:} and @code{afterfib-n-2:}, the base case announces
@code{immediate-answer:}, and the count stays undisturbed (fib 3 traced still
counts 51).
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.18}Exercise 5.18:} Modify the simulator so that
registers can be traced: each write to a traced register, by the host's
@code{set_register} or by @code{Assign}, @code{Assign_op}, or @code{Restore},
appends @code{name: old -> new} to the transcript.  Tracing @code{n} and
@code{val} through the factorial machine on @code{n = 3} opens with the
host's input load (@code{n: unassigned -> 3}) and ends with @code{fact 3 =
6}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.19}Exercise 5.19:} Alyssa P. Hacker wants a
@newterm{breakpoint} feature in the simulator to help her debug her machine
designs.  You have been hired to install this feature for her.  She wants to be
able to specify a place in the controller sequence where the simulator will
stop and allow her to examine the state of the machine.  You are to implement a
procedure

@example ocaml
set_breakpoint machine "test-b" 4
@end example

@noindent
that sets a breakpoint just before the @math{n^{\hbox{th}}} instruction after the given
label, counted from one.  For example,

@example ocaml
set_breakpoint gcd_machine "test-b" 4
@end example

@noindent
installs a breakpoint in @code{gcd-machine} just before the assignment to
register @code{a}.  When the simulator reaches the breakpoint it stops and
lets her use @code{get_register} and @code{set_register} to manipulate the
state of the simulated machine.  She should then be able to continue execution
by saying

@example ocaml
proceed_machine gcd_machine
@end example

@noindent
She should also be able to remove a specific breakpoint by means of

@example ocaml
cancel_breakpoint gcd_machine "test-b" 4
@end example

@noindent
or to remove all breakpoints by means of

@example ocaml
cancel_all_breakpoints gcd_machine
@end example

@noindent
On gcd(12, 8) the run stops at @code{test-b 4} with @code{a = 12},
@code{b = 8}, proceeds to stop there again with @code{a = 8}, @code{b = 4},
and proceeds to complete with @code{a = 4}.
@end quotation
